{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>And I fell down before HaShem as at the first, forty days and forty nights.</b> What Rashi explained here — 'and HaShem said to Moses: I have pardoned according to your word' — is not the verse written in the section of Shelach about the spies, 'and HaShem said: I have pardoned according to your word,' but rather it is Rashi's own language. For it is established with us that on the seventeenth of Tammuz, immediately after the Exodus from Egypt, the calf was made, and the spies were not sent until the twenty-ninth of Sivan thereafter." "<b>As at the first.</b> It does not refer to 'and I fell down,' for he had not yet sought mercy for them regarding the calf, but rather it refers to 'forty days and forty nights.' That is to say: I stood on the mountain <b>forty days and forty nights</b> without eating or drinking, as I did the first time. And these forty days began from the start of the night of the seventh day, on the eighteenth of Tammuz, until the start of the night of the fifth day, the twenty-ninth of Av. And during these forty days he did not ascend the mountain but prayed in his tent that he pitched for himself outside the camp, as he explained in the section of Tisa."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {0 "The phrase 'immediately after the Exodus from Egypt' (תיכף ליצ״מ) is difficult, since the Exodus from Egypt took place in Nisan, not Tammuz. I have translated hyper-literally; the text may be corrupt or may carry an idiom not readily apparent. The chronological argument itself — that the calf preceded the spies — also seems internally inconsistent with the dates cited."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:52:16.004960764Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}